WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.042 --> 00:00:01.751
This just in.

00:00:01.751 --> 00:00:03.086
NTSB investigators

00:00:03.086 --> 00:00:04.212
say four bolts

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:05.255
that hold the door

00:00:05.255 --> 00:00:08.049
plug in place on the Boeing 737

00:00:08.049 --> 00:00:09.551
max, nine were missing

00:00:09.551 --> 00:00:11.094
at the time of last month's

00:00:11.094 --> 00:00:14.097
blowout on Alaska Airlines Flight 1282.

00:00:14.305 --> 00:00:15.849
The near disaster happened

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:17.475
midair during a flight from Oregon

00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:21.521
to California, 177 people were on board.

00:00:21.604 --> 00:00:22.647
FAA chief Mike

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:24.315
Whitaker has been testifying today

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:24.816
on Capitol

00:00:24.816 --> 00:00:26.568
Hill about his oversight

00:00:26.568 --> 00:00:28.695
of Boeing following the incident.

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:30.405
We have seen an aviation correspondent,

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:32.115
Pete Monteith, here with us.

00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:32.365
All right.

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:34.325
Tell us more about his testimony

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:35.326
and this new report.

00:00:35.326 --> 00:00:35.952
Obviously,

00:00:35.952 --> 00:00:37.662
those four bolts were very important.

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:38.621
This is a bombshell

00:00:38.621 --> 00:00:40.206
finding from the National Transportation

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:40.749
Safety Board,

00:00:40.749 --> 00:00:42.333
who just released this 19 page

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:43.918
report, its preliminary report

00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:46.129
about that blowout back on January 5th.

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:47.464
And it essentially says

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:48.006
the Bolts,

00:00:48.006 --> 00:00:49.090
which was have been the focus

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:50.925
of this investigation from the start,

00:00:50.925 --> 00:00:51.676
were not there.

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:53.011
This is the actual type of bolt.

00:00:53.011 --> 00:00:53.553
This is an

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.056
and six bolt pretty common in aviation.

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:57.140
This is the bolt itself.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.808
There's a castle nut here

00:00:58.808 --> 00:00:59.768
and then a cotter pin

00:00:59.768 --> 00:01:01.227
to keep this all together.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:02.145
At one point,

00:01:02.145 --> 00:01:02.979
there was some conjecture

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:04.814
that maybe the Cotter pin was missing

00:01:04.814 --> 00:01:05.982
that would cause this castle

00:01:05.982 --> 00:01:08.485
not to work itself free.

00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:10.862
In actuality, the NTSB says

00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:13.239
none of these bolts were in place

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.325
at the time of this blowout.

00:01:15.325 --> 00:01:15.992
Last month,

00:01:15.992 --> 00:01:18.286
the NTSB was able to determine this

00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:19.245
because they were able

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:20.497
to recover the door

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:21.790
plug that fell off Alaska

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:22.999
Flight 1282

00:01:22.999 --> 00:01:23.917
in the Portland

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.710
backyard of a physics teacher.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.254
They brought it to their lab in D.C.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:29.005
to do something

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:30.548
called destructive testing.

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:32.425
And they were able to tell

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.761
from the telltale signs

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:36.054
on the door itself

00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:37.388
and also on the fittings.

00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:37.639
There

00:01:37.639 --> 00:01:38.890
you could see those

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:40.725
on the side of the door there

00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:42.811
that these bolts were simply

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:43.686
not installed.

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:45.480
The running theory from the NTSB

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.857
now is that these bolts were taken out

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:48.650
at some point

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:50.527
when this plane was very new

00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:52.737
at first flew at the end of October.

00:01:52.737 --> 00:01:54.489
But the fuselage was delivered

00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:57.492
by a subcontractor to Boeing on September

00:01:57.492 --> 00:02:00.912
1st of 2023 at Boeing.

00:02:00.995 --> 00:02:01.579
Technicians

00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:04.082
there noticed some issues with rivets

00:02:04.082 --> 00:02:05.458
next to the door,

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:06.417
and they were able

00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:08.378
to remove parts of the door

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:09.712
and door plug.

00:02:09.712 --> 00:02:12.799
And the NTSB was able to tell from photos

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:14.384
taken of the work

00:02:14.384 --> 00:02:16.678
that took place on September 19th

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:19.472
that the bolts were not installed then.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.725
This only adds further scrutiny

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.185
to Boeing,

00:02:24.185 --> 00:02:25.270
which is really under

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:26.437
a lot of pressure right now

00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:28.606
when it comes to its quality control.

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:29.858
And the big question here

00:02:29.858 --> 00:02:31.818
is why they removed those bolts

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.945
and why they did not put them back in.

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:35.238
That is something that the NTSB

00:02:35.238 --> 00:02:36.614
is still trying to determine.

00:02:36.614 --> 00:02:37.615
They want to figure out

00:02:37.615 --> 00:02:39.909
what the instructions were specifically

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:42.245
that were in the instruction manuals

00:02:42.245 --> 00:02:43.329
there for Boeing workers

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.206
at its plant in Renton, Washington.

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:45.665
Right now,

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:47.375
we just heard from the FAA administrator.

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:48.793
There are about two dozen

00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:50.295
FAA inspectors there

00:02:50.295 --> 00:02:51.796
conducting an audit of Boeing's

00:02:51.796 --> 00:02:52.839
quality control.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:54.507
But this is only the beginning.

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.467
This is just the preliminary report.

00:02:56.467 --> 00:02:57.677
We will see a final report

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:59.053
probably in a year's time.

00:02:59.053 --> 00:02:59.679
No laying

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.472
blame here by the NTSB on Boeing,

00:03:01.472 --> 00:03:02.515
although we could find that in

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:03.224
a final report.

00:03:03.224 --> 00:03:04.350
So just very quickly,

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:05.018
basically,

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:06.352
when this plane was pretty new,

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.229
yes, the door plug had to come off.

00:03:08.229 --> 00:03:10.064
Yes. A reason, a rivet reason.

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:12.400
And when it was put back on the bolts

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:15.195
and the issue is the door plug itself,

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:15.820
which you can't see

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:16.696
from the inside of the airplane,

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:17.405
you can only see it from

00:03:17.405 --> 00:03:18.448
the outside of the airplane,

00:03:18.448 --> 00:03:20.491
goes through so many cycles

00:03:20.491 --> 00:03:22.911
that the inside of the airplane expands

00:03:22.911 --> 00:03:23.953
because of pressurization

00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:24.704
and then contracts

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:26.206
when it's not pressurized.

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:27.707
So it's to hold it there in place.

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.417
But at a certain point,

00:03:29.417 --> 00:03:30.877
essentially, this door just blew free.

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:32.128
And the NTSB says

00:03:32.128 --> 00:03:34.172
the door blew out and up

00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:35.840
and free of the airplane

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.926
in a very explosive and dramatic way.

00:03:37.926 --> 00:03:39.427
We have seen the videos from on board.

00:03:39.427 --> 00:03:40.428
It is pretty incredible

00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:41.512
that everybody survived this

00:03:41.512 --> 00:03:42.972
and nobody was seriously hurt.

00:03:42.972 --> 00:03:44.390
Pete, thank you so much for that.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:45.808
I do want to bring in CNN safety

00:03:45.808 --> 00:03:47.143
analyst and former FAA

00:03:47.143 --> 00:03:48.770
safety inspector David Susi.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:53.024
Now, that is a bombshell, as Pete

00:03:53.024 --> 00:03:53.983
said there,

00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:54.859
especially Lee,

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:56.444
since it seemed before

00:03:56.444 --> 00:03:57.987
this door plug blew off.

00:03:57.987 --> 00:03:59.280
It was an airplane

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.240
that was kind of giving

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.366
some signs, right,

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:02.659
with the pressurization

00:04:02.659 --> 00:04:04.452
issues that it had been experiencing

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:06.871
on multiple occasions.

00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:07.914
You know,

00:04:07.914 --> 00:04:09.165
three times before this,

00:04:09.165 --> 00:04:11.084
they had pressurization indications

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:12.877
and the airplane was put on the ground.

00:04:12.877 --> 00:04:14.504
They inspected it, they checked it.

00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:15.588
They didn't go to the extent

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:16.673
of doing a pressurization

00:04:16.673 --> 00:04:17.715
check on the ground,

00:04:17.715 --> 00:04:18.925
which typically isn't done.

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:20.051
They'll do a couple of other things

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:21.219
before they do that.

00:04:21.219 --> 00:04:22.679
But that's the one thing that I think

00:04:22.679 --> 00:04:23.638
is missing here,

00:04:23.638 --> 00:04:25.306
that this problem started long,

00:04:25.306 --> 00:04:27.225
long before this actual door came out,

00:04:27.225 --> 00:04:29.560
that's for sure, at Boeing.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.271
What does this tell you, David,

00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:34.065
about what happened here and how?

00:04:34.065 --> 00:04:34.399
I mean,

00:04:34.399 --> 00:04:35.108
do you understand

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:35.984
how this could have happened

00:04:35.984 --> 00:04:37.777
that this door plug was put back on

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:41.864
without the bolts being there?

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:42.740
Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.075
I've been an aircraft mechanic,

00:04:44.075 --> 00:04:45.743
licensed mechanic for 45 years.

00:04:45.743 --> 00:04:46.953
I've worked on the line

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:48.871
installing aircraft

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:50.832
on the manufacturing line

00:04:50.832 --> 00:04:52.750
and there's often a problem

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:53.793
between the communication,

00:04:53.793 --> 00:04:55.503
especially in shift changes

00:04:55.503 --> 00:04:57.380
where they change from one to the other.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:57.839
But here's

00:04:57.839 --> 00:05:00.967
the situation is manufacturing employs

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:02.302
a certain mindset

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:04.387
when that might a manufacturing process,

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:07.307
the assembly line process is interrupted

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:08.975
with the nonconforming part,

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.184
which as Pete mentioned,

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:11.019
there were rivets

00:05:11.019 --> 00:05:12.270
that weren't installed properly

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.396
that had to be repaired.

00:05:13.396 --> 00:05:14.439
At that point,

00:05:14.439 --> 00:05:16.190
they're supposed to change their mindset

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:17.775
from being an assembly line

00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:18.985
to being a repair station.

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:20.528
They're actually performing repairs,

00:05:20.528 --> 00:05:23.114
which require additional inspections

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:24.866
when there's a safety of flight item.

00:05:24.866 --> 00:05:25.658
They have to have someone

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.368
who did not work on that airplane

00:05:27.368 --> 00:05:28.578
come in and do an inspection

00:05:28.578 --> 00:05:29.871
to make sure it's ready to go.

00:05:29.871 --> 00:05:31.956
So my question is not only why weren't

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:32.915
the bolts put in,

00:05:32.915 --> 00:05:34.208
but why did that process

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:35.835
not happen at Boeing?

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:36.461
And that's what

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:38.921
the FAA needs to make their focus on

00:05:38.921 --> 00:05:39.756
is what's going on

00:05:39.756 --> 00:05:40.757
at Boeing in that culture

00:05:40.757 --> 00:05:42.633
that would have allowed that to happen.

00:05:42.633 --> 00:05:44.260
Yeah, huge questions

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:45.011
about Boeing's

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.221
culture that have persisted for years

00:05:47.221 --> 00:05:49.015
and are ballooning again now.

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:50.141
David, great to have you.

00:05:50.141 --> 00:05:52.602
Thank you so much.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:53.186
Thanks for having.

